{
"level": "B1",
"el": "Δύο αντωνυμίες μαζί",
"title": "Two pronouns together",
"body": [
{
"p": "When there are two weak pronouns, the **genitive** (to whom) comes **first**, then the **accusative** (what):"
},
{
"ex": [
{
"el": "Μου το έδωσε.",
"en": "He gave it to me."
},
{
"el": "Θα σου τα στείλω.",
"en": "I'll send them to you."
},
{
"el": "Δεν της το είπα.",
"en": "I didn't tell her (it)."
}
]
},
{
"p": "In speech **σου το** often shortens to **σ' το**: **Θα σ' το πω** \"I'll tell you (it)\"."
},
{
"p": "After a positive imperative both orders are heard: **Δώσε μου το** and **Δώσ' το μου** \"Give it to me\". **Φέρ' το μου, Πες το μου, Στείλ' τα μου** are very common in speech."
},
{
"ex": [
{
"el": "Δώσ' το μου!",
"en": "Give it to me!"
},
{
"el": "Πες μου το ξανά.",
"en": "Tell me (it) again."
},
{
"el": "Μη μου το λες!",
"en": "Don't tell me (that)!"
}
]
},
{
"solved": [
{
"q": "\"I'll bring it to you.\" (informal, το)",
"a": "Θα σου το φέρω. | Θα σ' το φέρω.",
"why": "Genitive first, then accusative, before the verb."
},
{
"q": "\"She didn't give them to us.\" (τα)",
"a": "Δεν μας τα έδωσε.",
"why": "δεν + μας + τα + verb."
},
{
"q": "\"Tell it to me!\" (informal)",
"a": "Πες μου το! | Πες το μου!",
"why": "After the imperative both orders are used."
}
]
},
{
"check": [
{
