"id": "personal",
"el": "Προσωπικές αντωνυμίες",
"title": "Personal pronouns",
"span": "A1–B2",
"part": "Μέρος Δ΄ | Αντωνυμίες, αριθμητικά, προθέσεις | Pronouns, numbers and prepositions",
"blurb": "Strong and weak pronouns, where the weak ones go (before the verb, after an imperative), the order of two pronouns, and the doubling that makes Greek sound natural.",
"intro": [
{
"p": "Greek has two sets of personal pronouns:"
},
{
"list": [
"**strong** forms (**εγώ, εμένα, εσύ, εσένα, αυτός...**) for emphasis, contrast, after prepositions and on their own;",
"**weak** forms (**με, σε, τον, μου, σου, του...**), short unstressed words that lean on the verb. They are far more frequent."
]
},
{
"p": "As a subject the pronoun is usually left out, because the verb ending already shows the person: **Πεινάω** \"I'm hungry\". Say **εγώ** only for contrast: **Εγώ πεινάω, εσύ;** \"I'm hungry, what about you?\""
},
